# Transpose a score

`scripts/transpose_musicxml.py` needs only `python3` (standard library). It
changes pitches, key signatures, chord symbols and ficta marks; durations,
lyrics, beams, ties, breaks and layout are copied unchanged. Do it on the XML,
before import: the spelling is then decided once, in the file, and survives
every later step (breaks, tidy, export).

```bash
python3 "${CLAUDE_SKILL_DIR}/scripts/transpose_musicxml.py" in.xml out.xml --by "up M2" --report
python3 "${CLAUDE_SKILL_DIR}/scripts/transpose_musicxml.py" in.xml out.xml --by "down m3" --fifths 1
python3 "${CLAUDE_SKILL_DIR}/scripts/transpose_musicxml.py" in.xml out.xml --by "up M2" --octave Bassus=1
```

| Option | Meaning |
|---|---|
| `--by "up M2"` | Direction and interval: `m2 M2 m3 M3 P4 A4 d5 P5 m6 M6 m7 M7 P8`, `up` or `down` |
| `--fifths N` | Key signature to write (default: the old key shifted by the interval) |
| `--keep-key` | Leave key signatures alone and spell with accidentals |
| `--octave PART=N` | After transposing, move one part (id or name) by whole octaves |
| `--report` | Lowest and highest note of each part before and after |

## Steps

1. **Choose the interval from the singers, not the page.** Run with
   `--report` and read the ranges. A transposition that is wrong for one part
   (a bass that now sits at D2) needs a decision: `--octave Bassus=1` moves
   the whole line, but a director may prefer to double only the low notes at
   the octave, which is a manual edit after import (see below).
2. **Key signature.** The default shifts the old key (B-flat major, -2, up a
   major second gives 0). If the music is modal and you want a different
   signature, pass `--fifths`. Check that the notes look right in the
   editor; mid-piece key changes are all shifted.
3. **Ficta.** The marks name the accidental the note now has, so a ficta
   natural on B in B-flat major (a B natural) becomes a ficta sharp on C
   (up a major second, no signature). The script keeps ficta one-note-only:
   the next note of the same letter returns to the key signature. Many
   modern transcribers carry an accidental to the end of the bar instead;
   a hand transposition of the Brumel Du tout plongiet differed from this
   script at exactly the 7 notes that follow a ficta in the same bar. Decide
   which convention the choir expects and say so in the score or the
   delivery note; do not mix them.
4. **Import and check.** Import, then compare the result with the source
   (or someone else's transposition) bar by bar: sounding pitches, ficta,
   and range. Use `score-polish-for-sharing` for the rest of the chain; run
   transposing before the phrase-break and layout steps.
5. **Range fixes after import.** Octave doublings (for example the first two
   Bassus bars doubled a D3 above the D2) are editorial: add them with
   `add-note` in the editor and tell the user which bars you changed.

## Checked against

Brumel, Du tout plongiet (4 parts, 62 bars, B-flat to no signature, up a
major second): the script's spelling matches a hand transposition in 700 of
709 notes. The 9 differences are all ficta handling: 7 notes that follow a
ficta in the same bar (above) and 2 ficta that the hand version leaves out.

## Limits

- Only `score-partwise`; percussion and unpitched notes are left alone.
- A `<transpose>` element (a transposing instrument) is not interpreted:
  the file is treated as concert pitch.
- Mixed enharmonic spelling follows the interval, not the key: a note that
  needs a double accidental in the new key is written with one.
- The editors can also transpose a score they have open (see the `sibelius`,
  `dorico` and `musescore` skills); use this script when the file is not
  imported yet or the spelling must stay under your control.
